<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matanzas Inlet</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas">Matanzas</a> Inlet is a channel linking the Matanzas River to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>, about 23 km south of St Augustine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. In September and October 1565 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a> had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-ribault">Jean Ribault</a> and several hundred shipwrecked French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> executed there, which gave the inlet its name, Spanish for 'slaughters'. Fort Matanzas, built by the Spanish in 1740–1742, guards it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matanzas-inlet.jpg" alt="Matanzas Inlet" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Image courtesy of Janet Zimmerman at Florida Inland Navigation District · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matanzas_Inlet_Aerial_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The inlet runs between two barrier islands and the mainland at the south end of the Matanzas River, in the southern part of St. Johns County. It is not stabilised by jetties and so is subject to shifting: maps made by Spanish military engineers in the 18th century show that it has moved many hundreds of yards south of where it lay in the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>. It is the last remaining natural, undredged inlet on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name goes back to the contest between the French and the Spanish over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. René Goulaine de Laudonnière founded Fort Caroline, in what is now Jacksonville, in 1564 as a haven for Huguenot settlers. In response to this encroachment on what <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> regarded as its territory, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a> founded St. Augustine in 1565 and soon set out overland to attack Fort Caroline, while the French sailed from the fort intending to attack St. Augustine from the sea.</p><p>The Spanish overwhelmed the lightly defended Fort Caroline, sparing only the women and children, although some 25 men escaped. A storm drove the French fleet off course, and many of its ships were wrecked on the coast south of St. Augustine. When the Spanish found the main group of shipwreck survivors, Menéndez de Avilés ordered all of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> executed, and the place became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas">Matanzas</a>.</p><p>The inlet was the southernmost access for boat travel between St. Augustine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>. In 1740 a British invasion force from Fort Frederica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> blockaded it, and shortly afterwards the Spanish authorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> built a coquina stone tower, 50 feet square and 30 feet high, to safeguard the inlet. The tower is now called Fort Matanzas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matanzas Inlet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1565 Philip II sent Pedro Menéndez de Avilés, who founded St Augustine, captured Fort Caroline and executed Ribault and his shipwrecked men at <b>Matanzas Inlet</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-florida">French Florida</a></li></ul></section>
